RULE NUMBER
014

TITLE
Forge: Add new catalog kind to catalog_kind.proto enum

ROLE
You are the Planton Code Partner working inside the developer's local workspace in Cursor. This rule ensures that newly forged catalog kinds are properly registered in the `catalog_kind.proto` enum with correct provider ranges and metadata.

SCOPE
- Add new enum entry to `shared/catalogkind/catalog_kind.proto`
- Determine correct enum value based on provider range
- Generate appropriate id_prefix following naming conventions
- Assign the kind's service_group from the provider's group list
- Do not modify other forge-generated files

PROVIDER RANGES
The catalog_kind.proto uses 1,000-wide provider bands (the
authoritative statement is the band comment table at the top of the enum
itself -- read it if this list ever disagrees):
- Test/dev/custom: 1-49
- SaaS platforms: 50-199
- AWS: 1000-1999
- Azure: 2000-2999
- GCP: 3000-3999
- Kubernetes: 4000-4999
- DigitalOcean: 5000-5999
- Cloudflare: 7000-7999
- Auth0: 8000-8999
- OpenFGA: 9000-9999
- Stripe: 10000-10999

Providers organize their bands into FAMILY SUB-BANDS (see the `--- ... ---`
comments inside each band). When a wave of related kinds is planned, its
sub-band is allocated deliberately at wave start -- never grab numbers ad
hoc inside someone else's family block. Enum numbers are pinned by the
registry snapshot: after adding entries, regenerate
`pkg/catalogkindreflect/testdata/registry_numbers.txt` with
`go test ./pkg/catalogkindreflect/ -run TestRegistryNumberSnapshot -update` in the
same change.

ID PREFIX PATTERNS
- AWS: the "aws" head + a 2-5 char abbreviation (e.g., "awsvpc", "awsecr", "awss3")
- Azure: typically "az" prefix + 2-6 char abbreviation (e.g., "aks", "azdns", "azkv")
- GCP: typically "gcp" prefix + 3-7 char abbreviation (e.g., "gcpdns", "gcpsql", "gcpcert")
- Kubernetes: the "k8s" head + an abbreviation of the kind (e.g., "k8sdpl", "k8sgoff", "k8sflagd"); a content kind that serves an engine kind extends the engine's abbreviation ("k8sgofff" for KubernetesGoFeatureFlagFlagFile)
- DigitalOcean: "do" prefix + abbreviation (e.g., "doapp", "dodns", "docert")
- Cloudflare: "cf" prefix + abbreviation (e.g., "cfdns", "cfkvn", "cfr2b")
- Auth0: "a0" prefix + abbreviation (e.g., "a0cli", "a0conn", "a0es")
- Stripe: "stp" prefix + abbreviation of the Stripe object. A multi-word object takes its initials ("stpwh" webhook endpoint, "stpbpc" billing portal configuration, "stppmc" payment method configuration, "stped" event destination, "stpef" entitlement feature); a one-word object is spelled out, shortened to at most five letters, so two one-word objects never read one letter apart ("stpprod" product, "stpprice" price); when two Stripe objects share initials, each takes Stripe's own id prefix with its vowels dropped instead ("stptxr" tax rate, from txr_; "stptxrg" tax registration, from taxreg_; both would otherwise be "stptr")

SEQUENTIAL STEPS
1) Identify: Determine the provider and kind name from the resource being forged
   - Provider from directory path (e.g., `catalog/gcp/gcpcertmanagercert` → gcp)
   - Kind name from directory name in PascalCase (e.g., `gcpcertmanagercert` → GcpCertManagerCert)

2) Find Next Enum Value:
   - Read `shared/catalogkind/catalog_kind.proto`
   - Identify provider's range from PROVIDER RANGES above
   - Scan existing entries in that range to find the highest used number
   - Next value = highest + 1
   - Ensure it's within the provider's range

3) Generate ID Prefix:
   - The prefix is embedded in EVERY resource id (`ic_<prefix>_<ulid>`) for
     the resource's lifetime and is the id's only kind discriminator — the
     CLI, search lookups, and kind auto-detection all resolve the kind from
     it. Every character rides every id forever, so the budget is hard:
     * lowercase letters/digits only — NEVER an underscore (it is the id
       separator the parsers split on)
     * starts with the provider's tag (gcp/aws/az/k8s...) so any id
       attributes its provider at a glance
     * at most 8 characters total; target 5-7
   - Check for uniqueness against existing id_prefix values (the registry's
     prefix index hard-fails on duplicates — `go test ./pkg/catalogkindreflect/`
     is the mechanical gate)
   - Examples:
     * GcpCertManagerCert → "gcpcert"
     * GcpSecretManagerSecret → "gcpsec"
     * AwsSecurityGroup → "awssg"
     * AzureKeyVault → "azkv"

4) Choose the Service Group:
   - Every kind of a grouped provider (aws, azure, gcp, kubernetes,
     cloudflare, digital_ocean) MUST carry exactly one `service_group` —
     the provider-console category the kind is browsed under. The
     registry coherence test fails the build on a missing group, a group
     belonging to a different provider, or a group on an ungrouped
     provider (auth0, openfga).
   - Pick from the provider's values in
     `shared/catalogkind/catalog_provider_service_group.proto` —
     choose the group the provider's own console files the underlying
     service under (e.g. EKS → aws_containers, Key Vault →
     azure_security). Sub-resources stay with their parent service's
     group. For Kubernetes: native API objects →
     kubernetes_core_workloads_apis; cluster machinery/operators →
     kubernetes_operators_controllers; applications deployed on the
     cluster → kubernetes_open_source_software.
   - Adding a NEW group value to that enum is rare and deliberate — it is
     a proto-lock choke-point edit and needs its `service_group_meta`
     (provider + Title Case display_name) plus console-side label
     convergence; prefer an existing group.

5) Insert Enum Entry:
   - Add the new enum value in numerical order within the provider's section
   - Use the format:
     ```proto
     <KindName> = <number> [(kind_meta) = {
       provider: <provider_name>
       version: "v1alpha1"
       id_prefix: "<generated_prefix>"
       service_group: <provider_group>
     }];
     ```
   - For kinds that project a single Kubernetes custom resource, add
     kubernetes_manifest_projection if applicable
   - For a kind that deliberately runs on fewer engines than both, add
     `provisioners` with the engines it runs on, by IacProvisioner value
     name: `provisioners: ["tofu"]` for a kind proven on OpenTofu alone,
     `["tofu", "terraform"]` for a provider with no Pulumi provider. Leave
     it out for the ordinary kind with both modules. The anatomy gate then
     holds the kind's iac/ folder to exactly the modules those engines
     need, and the CLI and the platform refuse any other engine
   - Maintain consistent indentation (2 spaces)

6) Regenerate the Kind Reflection Map (MANDATORY):
   - Run from the repo root: `make generate-catalog-kind-map`
   - This regenerates `pkg/catalogkindreflect/kind_map_gen.go`, the enum -> proto-message
     map (`ToMessageMap`) that manifest loading, chart validation, and the E2E
     harness all resolve kinds through. Without it, the new kind's manifests
     fail to load ("UNSUPPORTED CATALOG KIND") even though the enum
     entry and stubs exist -- and no proto build step catches the omission.
   - `make build` runs this target, but rule 015's `make protos` does NOT --
     so run it here, immediately after the enum edit.

7) Validation:
   - Verify no duplicate enum values
   - Verify no duplicate id_prefix values
   - Verify enum value is in correct provider range
   - Verify the service_group belongs to the kind's provider
     (`go test ./pkg/catalogkindreflect/` runs the coherence and coverage gates)
   - Verify formatting matches existing entries
   - Verify the new kind appears in `pkg/catalogkindreflect/kind_map_gen.go`
   - Verify every reference the spec makes into a container kind (a
     namespace, a VPC, a subnet, a project) has a containment verdict:
     `go test ./shared/catalogkind/` lists each new one. `contained`
     means the resource lives inside the target, so diagrams nest it there;
     a reference to something the resource only reads or watches (the
     namespaces a monitor scrapes, a project a store reads secrets from) is
     access-style and carries `(dev.planton.shared.foreignkey.v1.containment_exempt) = true`
     on its field. Review the listed verdicts, fix any wrong one on the field,
     then regenerate the golden file with `-update`.

SUCCESS
- New enum entry added to catalog_kind.proto
- Enum value is unique and in correct provider range
- ID prefix is unique and follows naming convention
- service_group assigned from the kind's own provider (grouped providers)
- Entry is inserted in numerical order
- Formatting matches existing entries
- File is syntactically correct

OUTPUT
- Confirm the added enum entry with its number, id_prefix, and service_group
- Show the provider range and position within that range
- Example: "Added GcpCertManagerCert = 3019 with id_prefix 'gcpcert' and service_group gcp_security (GCP range: 3000-3999)"

NOTES
- This rule should be run after api.proto is created (rule 005)
- The enum number should never be changed once assigned
- The id_prefix should be globally unique across all providers
- If the provider's range is full, alert the developer to expand the range
